# CHANGELOG

## 20260824

0.15.2: Run git add and git commit against the repository, not the working directory.

1. ~ main(): git add -A and git commit ran wherever the process happened to be, so the import only worked when run from inside the target repository. The rm and cp used absolute paths and were unaffected, which is why it looked like it worked. Both git commands now take git -C #{repo}.
2. It had never worked from elsewhere: the dead `run "cd ..."` removed at 0.15.0 was the last trace of an attempt to set the directory, and a subshell cd could not have carried across separate run calls anyway.
3. Met importing the Browser library, whose repository sat beside the invocation rather than beneath it.
4. ~ bin/git-import-all: /0.15.1/0.15.2/


0.15.1: Set the author date alone, the committer date having been stated as a day the commit was not made upon.

1. ~ bin/git-import-all: ~ commit_environment(): - GIT_COMMITTER_DATE. The author date is when the work was authored, which a CHANGELOG knows to the day; the committer date is when the commit was made, which is during the import. Setting both from the entry stated that every imported commit was made upon a day it was not, sometimes years earlier. Left unset, git takes the time of the commit, which is what filter-branch, rebase, am and cherry-pick each do: keep the author date, take a fresh committer date.
2. It shows in little, which is why it stood: `git log` prints one date and it is the author's, so the false one is reached only through `--pretty=fuller`, `%ci`, or a sort by commit date.
3. The cost is reproducibility. An import run twice over the same revisions now yields different commits where before it yielded the same ones. A switch could have that back where it is wanted; a false date by default is too much to pay for it.
4. Nothing already imported is corrected by this. The commits which carry it are fixed only by rewriting them, which for a pushed repository means every SHA moving and every tag re-pointing, to correct a field almost nothing reads. tap-audit's fifteen were rewritten because they were unpushed and free to rewrite.


0.15.0: - two requires, the git-boot branch and repo_dir, each left by a decision already taken.

1. - require 'fileutils': dead since 0.2.3, which routed shell work through Kernel#run and replaced every FileUtils call with a shelled-out one. The require stayed.
2. - require 'impuri': never used, in any commit, under either name. It arrived at 0.5.0 with the rename from git-importall, which carried a require block forward wholesale.
3. - the git-boot branch in main(): unreachable since 0.4.0, "Make it work locally only, since we have git push for the other bit", which gave repo_dir its nil-returning form. repository_exist?(nil) raises TypeError before the branch body runs, so `mkdir -p`, `cd` and `git-boot` have not run since. The `cd` was doing nothing regardless, a subshell's working directory being its own.
4. + a refusal in its place, naming the directory and saying that this imports into a repository rather than creating one, where it raised `TypeError: no implicit conversion of nil into String`, which names nothing. It refused before and refuses now, both with exit 1: only the legibility changes.
5. - repo_dir(): it held the existence check, which has moved into main() where it is acted upon. What was left returned its argument, and both call sites are within main(repo), where repo is already named.
6. A minor rather than a patch because the reference to git-boot goes, and with it the claim that this creates the repository it imports into. Whether that path ran is beside the point: 0.1.0 added it deliberately — "Issue a git-boot if the remote repository does not exist" — and anyone reading the source would have believed it. Removing a capability the program stated is a minor however long it had been broken.
7. ~ git-import-all.rb in the tap: - resource 'impuri', a gem fetched, checksummed and installed upon every build for a require never once exercised, and with it the whole of the vendoring — GEM_HOME, GEM_PATH, resources.each(&:fetch), and a gem install whose argument list was the empty splat of no resources. RUBYLIB stays, lib/Kernel/run.rb being reached that way.
8. ~ bin/git-import-all: /0.14.3/0.15.0/, and the date.


## 20260823

0.14.3: A version line without a summary begins an entry, changelogger writing one bare.

1. ~ Changelog::Format::DatedHeading::VERSIONED_SUMMARY: the colon and the summary are optional. changelogger writes `0.3.0` bare where the revision it read carried a Changes section with no marker to take a summary from, and five of the Browser library's sixteen are written that way. Alone beneath its own heading such an entry parses, the heading separating it; sharing a heading with another it did not, having nothing to begin a block, and was folded into its neighbour.
2. ~ versioned_blocks(): a bare version line gives no summary, so the entry takes its first item instead, which is what entry() already does where a summary is absent. 0.3.0's is "Moved Browser::Base to own file."
3. Where it showed: the Browser library parsed to fourteen entries for sixteen revisions, then fifteen once changelogger 0.14.0 gave three headings their real dates, which moved 0.3.0 under a heading it shared. check_entry_count! refuses anything but agreement, so the import would not run at all.
4. Sixteen for sixteen now, in order, every version and date read.
5. ~ bin/git-import-all: /0.14.2/0.14.3/, and the date.


## 20260821

0.14.2: + LICENSE, which the README has named without one being present.

1. + LICENSE: the MIT text, copyright 2014-2026 thoran. The year is the CHANGELOG's earliest entry rather than the first commit: this history was imported from numbered revisions, which reach back further than the repository does. The README has answered "MIT" under a heading of its own while the repository carried no licence text at all, so anyone taking it at its word had nothing to read and nothing to comply with.
2. ~ bin/git-import-all: /0.14.1/0.14.2/, and the date.


0.14.1: - Dir/self.existQ, the platform having caught up with it.

1. - require 'Dir/self.existQ', - lib/Dir/self.existQ.rb. It defines Dir.exist? as File.directory?, and this called Dir.exist? in repository_exist?, so it was in use rather than dead: grepping for existQ finds nothing but the require, the use being through the name it overrides.
2. Why it goes: Dir.exist? has been a builtin since 1.9.2, and the two agree upon every case tried — a directory, a file, a symlink to a directory, a missing path, an empty string, nil, an Integer. So it substituted C for an identical Ruby copy, which is slower and a surprise in a backtrace.
3. Why it was right: written 2011.03.11, seven months after 1.9.2, but macOS shipped 1.8.7 as its system ruby until 2013 and 1.8.7 has no Dir.exist?. It was the implementation for the ruby actually to hand, and stopped being needed at some point across fourteen years without anything to mark the moment.
4. ~/lib/ruby keeps its copy at 0.0.1: this removes the vendored one, not the original.
5. Nothing else here wanted anything. audit_requires reports nothing missing, and vendor_requires would copy fourteen further files in which are not wanted, every one of those paths being answered by impuri, which the Gemfile declares and the formula vendors.
6. ~ bin/git-import-all: /0.14.0/0.14.1/


0.14.0: Read every version beneath a date heading, not only the first.

1. ~ Changelog.parse(): flat_map over the chunks, and the reverse moved off the chunks onto the flattened list. A heading standing over several versions holds them newest-first as the file does, so reversing the chunks alone left those in the wrong order.
2. ~ DatedHeading.parse(): an entry per versioned summary beneath the heading, rather than one entry for the heading. Where the body states no version it returns the single entry it did before, so a heading over one version parses exactly as it used to.
3. + DatedHeading.versioned_blocks(): the body split at each versioned summary, each block's text beginning with its own summary line so that entry() reads the items belonging to it.
4. + DatedHeading.unversioned_entry(): what 0.13.1 did, kept for a heading whose body names no version.
5. ~ VersionedHeading.parse(): returns its entry in a list, every format now returning one.
6. ~ CHANGELOG: one heading per date, thirty-six becoming thirteen. changelogger writes this form as of 0.10.0, and 20140510's fourteen versions said their date fourteen times.
7. moby's CHANGELOG was already in this form: forty-eight versions under twenty-two headings. Parsed by 0.13.1 it yielded twenty-two entries and dropped the other twenty-six without a word, so an import of it would have made twenty-two commits for forty-eight versions and check_entry_count! would have refused on a count nothing explained.


## 20260805

0.13.1: Quote the commit message, so that the shell cannot interpret it.

1. ~ main(): Escape the message with Shellwords rather than wrapping it in double quotes. A summary containing $PWD was expanded to the working directory; one containing backticks or $(...) would have been executed, and one containing a quote would have broken the command outright. Found while importing changelogger, whose 0.2.0 summary is "Implicit $PWD.".
2. + require 'shellwords'


0.13.0: Revisions are numbered directories.

1. + revision_directories(): Only numbered directories are taken as revisions, sorted by their number. The glob in main() took whatever sat in the revisions root, so a stray file was imported as a revision of its own. changelogger's version_directories() has always worked this way, and the two now agree on what a revision is.
2. ~ main(): Use revision_directories().
3. - require 'Array/natural_sortX', along with lib/Array/natural_sortX.rb and the lib/String/natural_comparison.rb which it requires. A natural sort exists to order names which might be arbitrary, and the filter has made that impossible; nothing else uses either file.
4. ~ README.md: Say that only numbered directories are taken as revisions, and that anything else in the revisions root is passed over.


0.12.0: Fill in .gitignore.

1. + .gitignore: The standard set of exclusions, git-boot's root commit having deliberately left the file empty. Nothing had in fact been ignored until now: .DS_Store and .claude stayed out of the imported commits only because git-import-all's glob does not match dotfiles, and would have been picked up by the first ordinary commit made in the repository.


0.11.0: Add a README, and retire the Changes section from the script header.

1. + README.md: What the tool is for, the revisions convention, how entries are matched to revisions by position, the two CHANGELOG formats recognised, the relationship to git-boot and changelogger, and a note on this repository having been bootstrapped by the tool itself.
2. - The Changes section from the script header. The CHANGELOG holds that record now, and keeping both invites them to disagree. Revisions 0 to 31 keep theirs, being the source from which the CHANGELOG was reconstructed and the only record of what changed for 0.1.0 onward.


0.10.1: Emit a commit date git will accept.

1. ~ commit_date(): Emit a time and a UTC offset, not just a date. git rejects a bare "2014-05-08" in GIT_AUTHOR_DATE with "fatal: invalid date format", so every commit failed. The T form is used rather than the spaced one so that no shell quoting is needed.


0.10.0: Refuse to import when the entry count and the revision count disagree.

1. + check_entry_count!(): Messages are matched by index, so a missing or extra entry silently shifts every message below it onto the wrong revision. Nothing else detects this, since each entry is individually well formed; there is simply one too few or too many.
2. ~ main(): Check before committing anything.


## 20260804

0.9.0: Commit with the date the CHANGELOG entry carries, rather than the date of the import.

1. + commit_date(): The CHANGELOG entry's date, normalised to ISO.
2. + commit_environment(): GIT_AUTHOR_DATE and GIT_COMMITTER_DATE for the commit, empty when the entry carries no date.
3. ~ main(): Commit with the entry's date.
4. + require 'date'


0.8.0: Dispatch each CHANGELOG entry to a named format, and fail rather than guess.

1. + module Changelog, with Changelog.parse() replacing parse_entries(). Inlined for now; to be extracted to a library once the format set is complete, so that changelogger can share it.
2. + Changelog::Format::DatedHeading: "## 20260726" with the version and summary on the body line, which is the style most of the repositories use.
3. + Changelog::Format::VersionedHeading: the version in the ## heading, covering the parenthesised, bracketed and em dashed variants.
4. + Changelog::Format.for(): Dispatch per entry, requiring exactly one format to match. Nothing matching, or more than one matching, raises rather than silently yielding a wrong message.
5. - heading_summary(), parse_entries().


## 20260731

0.7.0: Read a version and a date out of any of the CHANGELOG heading styles in use.

1. + heading_summary(): Strip the version and date out of a heading, leaving the summary.
2. ~ parse_entries(): Scan the ## heading for a version and a date independently, rather than matching one rigid form. Assignment is with ||=, so a later ## line within an entry cannot overwrite values already found.


## 20260504

0.6.0: Match each revision to its own CHANGELOG entry.

1. ~ read_changelog(): Find CHANGELOG or CHANGELOG.md from the highest numbered directory globally.
2. /parse_latest_entry/parse_entries/: Return all entries indexed oldest-first.
3. ~ commit_message(): Match revision index to corresponding CHANGELOG entry.
4. ~ main(): Parse entries once, pass index to commit_message().


